---
/**
 * The unlock block on every public ZIP page.
 *
 * Round 4 shipped this as a waitlist — "get told when your home dashboard is
 * ready" — because it wasn't. Round 5 built it, which made this copy false on
 * 225 indexed pages: it collected addresses for a product that already existed
 * and steered people away from the thing they were asking for.
 *
 * It is now a signup. Answer-first, like the rest of the indexed pages: it says
 * what the account gives you and what it costs (nothing), then links to
 * sign-in. Nothing is promised that Round 5 did not build — the three named
 * features are the reminder engine, the condition alerts and the saved home,
 * all of which exist. Municipal pickup days are deliberately not listed here,
 * because those are still Round 5b.
 *
 * The old capture endpoint and its table stay untouched: the people already on
 * that list asked to be told when this went live, and they are owed that
 * message rather than a silent deletion.
 */
// No props: Round 8's rewrite made this block ZIP-agnostic. It used to open
// with "the {areaLabel} reading above is the same for every home in {zip}" —
// which the copy doc removed on the grounds that leading a conversion pitch
// with the product's weakest fact is a poor trade.
---

{/* Round 8 honesty gate. The copy doc's conversion block leads with "Your
    house's weak points, flagged — enter your roof's age, your AC's age, and
    your foundation type", and the mid-page teaser repeats it. That mechanic
    does not exist: the Round 5 home profile stores an address, reminders and
    alert preferences, and nothing anywhere reads a component age or a
    foundation type, let alone flags a signal against one. Shipping it would
    promise behaviour the build does not have, so the paragraph is held until
    it is built and the block ships the three benefits that are real. The
    doc's "paper trail" line is kept — the dated history it describes is
    exactly what reminder_events and the alert log already record. */}
<section class="block alt" id="get-notified">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:640px;">
    <div class="card unlock-card">
      <p class="card-tag">Free account &middot; Two minutes</p>
      <h2>The score above is about your area. Let's make it about your house.</h2>
      <p>
        Conditions are only half the story — the other half is what they're landing on. A free
        account starts keeping track of your side of it:
      </p>
      <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.25em;">
        <li><strong>Alerts when it matters, quiet when it doesn't.</strong> Freeze, hail, extreme heat and flooding recorded for your county, each with what to check. You choose which ones you get.</li>
        <li><strong>Maintenance that runs itself.</strong> Filters, gutters, pest treatment, alarm tests. Mark one done and the next due date recalculates from that day. No spreadsheet, no sticky notes.</li>
        <li><strong>Your paper trail.</strong> The checks you log and the conditions we record become a dated history of your home — useful for an insurance claim, and for the day you sell.</li>
        <li><strong>Your ZIP and home saved</strong> &mdash; so the reading you want is the one you land on.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        No password — we email you a sign-in link. No credit card, no phone number, and we never
        sell your data. Area readings stay county-level; an account personalises what you track,
        not the precision of the data.
      </p>
      <p class="unlock-actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/home/sign-in/" data-signup-cta>Create my home's dashboard — free &rarr;</a>
      </p>
      <p class="notify-fineprint">
        Already set up? <a href="/home/">Open my dashboard</a> &middot;{" "}
        <a href="/privacy/">How we handle your data</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll("[data-signup-cta]").forEach((el) => {
    el.addEventListener("click", () => window.__thiTrack?.("signup_cta_clicked", {}));
  });
</script>
